From e77f282c8b73e988bd1f6aff803cfd93c97c3659 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Arya Alikhani <alexanderaryaalikhani@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 1 Oct 2026 20:28:23 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 13/13] boringssl: allow early data with record size limits

Patch 0005 disabled TLS 1.3 early data whenever RFC 8449 record size
limits were configured or negotiated. RFC 8449 section 4 says records are
subject to the limits set in the handshake that produced their keys. A
client sends 0-RTT before EncryptedExtensions carries the server's limit,
so early data records can only be bounded by the protocol maximum of
2^14+1 bytes of TLSInnerPlaintext.

Let a client that sends record_size_limit offer early data, and keep the
early-data capability of tickets from connections that negotiated a
limit. As in NSS, the whole 0-RTT write epoch, including EndOfEarlyData,
uses the protocol maximum; the server's limit applies from the handshake
write keys. A 0-RTT record still pending when that limit takes effect is
finished on the next write instead of failing with BAD_WRITE_RETRY.

A server that negotiates a limit below the protocol maximum cannot accept
such records without exceeding the limit it advertised, so it issues no
early-data tickets and declines 0-RTT with ssl_early_data_disabled, after
the HelloRetryRequest check. A server at the maximum accepts early data.
When a server declines 0-RTT, it skips undecryptable early records
without checking them against its negotiated limit; decrypted records
still face the plaintext limit.
